WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.992
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4408.

2
00:00:00.992 --> 00:00:01.984
Begin Session I.]

3
00:00:01.984 --> 00:00:10.252
<v DOUGLAS MUNGIN>So, today is January 24, 2015, and I'm Douglas Mungin, representing the T. Harry Williams Center for Oral History with LSU [Louisiana State University] Libraries.

4
00:00:10.252 --> 00:00:16.866
We are working in conjunction with the Imperial Calcasieu Museum and Bill Shearman to document the history of Mossville, Louisiana.

5
00:00:16.866 --> 00:00:24.142
And to kick that off, we are holding Mossville Oral History Day here at the Rigmaiden Recreation Center on Old Spanish Trail.

6
00:00:24.142 --> 00:00:25.465
I'm here today with--

7
00:00:25.465 --> 00:00:26.126
<v KENENTH LEE>Kenneth Lee.

8
00:00:26.126 --> 00:00:27.118
Arthur Kenneth Lee.

9
00:00:27.118 --> 00:00:29.433
<v MUNGIN>Thank you for agreeing to be interviewed.

10
00:00:29.433 --> 00:00:33.732
This is going to take about fifteen minutes long, and, like Jennifer [A.

11
00:00:33.732 --> 00:00:38.031
Cramer] said, we're going to probably follow up with you after this interview.

12
00:00:38.031 --> 00:00:42.000
Alright, so could you please state your full name once again?

13
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:43.824
<v LEE>Arthur Kenneth Lee.

14
00:00:43.824 --> 00:00:46.863
<v MUNGIN>And when were you born?

15
00:00:46.863 --> 00:00:48.686
<v LEE>September 26, 1948.

16
00:00:48.686 --> 00:00:51.725
<v MUNGIN>And where were you born?

17
00:00:51.725 --> 00:00:55.980
<v LEE>I was born in Lake Charles, Louisiana.

18
00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:59.627
<v MUNGIN>And what did your parents do?

19
00:00:59.627 --> 00:01:04.490
<v LEE>My parents were Richard and Elaine Lemelle Lee.

20
00:01:04.490 --> 00:01:11.176
My father was a plant worker for a number of years.

21
00:01:11.176 --> 00:01:13.000
<v MUNGIN>What plant?

22
00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:14.099
<v LEE>At Citgo.

23
00:01:14.099 --> 00:01:17.944
And back then, it was Cities Service.

24
00:01:17.944 --> 00:01:24.535
He also, after working at Cities Service for a number of years,

25
00:01:24.535 --> 00:01:31.676
he worked as an assistant veterinarian for Dr. Morris, a small animal hospital . . .

26
00:01:31.676 --> 00:01:32.775
<v MUNGIN>Where at?

27
00:01:32.775 --> 00:01:34.423
<v LEE>In Lake Charles.

28
00:01:34.423 --> 00:01:36.070
<v MUNGIN>In Lake Charles?

29
00:01:36.070 --> 00:01:39.366
And what does your mom do?

30
00:01:39.366 --> 00:01:52.000
<v LEE>My mom was a domestic, and she was like a LPN, a nurse, for the Garrison family, the Bells in Lake Charles.

31
00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:56.444
<v MUNGIN>And are your parents from the Lake Charles area, too?

32
00:01:56.444 --> 00:02:06.222
<v LEE>They are here from Mossville, and my father was from what they call Bayou D’Inde which is a little south from Mossville.

33
00:02:06.222 --> 00:02:08.444
It's, Highway 90 separates it.

34
00:02:08.444 --> 00:02:08.889
<v MUNGIN>Okay.

35
00:02:08.889 --> 00:02:12.000
And what are your grandparents' names?

36
00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:21.621
<v LEE>My mom's parents were Edward and Ida Mae Lemelle, and my grandmother was a Towner before marriage.

37
00:02:21.621 --> 00:02:31.808
My dad's daddy was a Richard-- I mean, my dad, Richard Lee, his father's name was Jim Lee,

38
00:02:31.808 --> 00:02:39.165
and his mother was named Alder, and she was an Olivier before marriage.

39
00:02:39.165 --> 00:02:43.126
<v MUNGIN>And are they also originally from Mossville?

40
00:02:43.126 --> 00:02:53.879
<v LEE>No, my grandfather was from Bayou D’Inde and my grandmother on my dad's side was from around [Grand Mary?].

41
00:02:53.879 --> 00:02:55.011
<v MUNGIN>Grand Mary.

42
00:02:55.011 --> 00:02:56.709
Where's Grand Mary?

43
00:02:56.709 --> 00:02:59.538
<v LEE>South of. . . Southeast of here.

44
00:02:59.538 --> 00:03:06.896
About twenty, thirty miles on the other side of, in St. Martin Parish.

45
00:03:06.896 --> 00:03:07.462
<v MUNGIN>Okay.

46
00:03:07.462 --> 00:03:13.121
So, could you give me your earliest memory of Mossville?

47
00:03:13.121 --> 00:03:14.819
Like, growing up?

48
00:03:14.819 --> 00:03:18.214
<v LEE>Man, that's, there's so many memories.

49
00:03:18.214 --> 00:03:28.401
There were times, my happiest times was riding horses and going to help with butchers on the weekends.

50
00:03:28.401 --> 00:03:32.363
We'd go pen cows and run cattle.

51
00:03:32.363 --> 00:03:41.984
Everybody had cattle, just about a cow or two, and they would kind of run wild there.

52
00:03:41.984 --> 00:03:46.511
There wasn’t any [cow?] pen laws back then.

53
00:03:46.511 --> 00:03:55.000
And cows was that large, and we would go get them and brand them . . .

54
00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:57.120
<v MUNGIN>What's cow pen laws?

55
00:03:57.120 --> 00:03:58.710
<v LEE>. . . in the spring.

56
00:03:58.710 --> 00:04:05.070
You know, where stock was running at large back on the bayou.

57
00:04:05.070 --> 00:04:09.310
We'd get together and go pen them up.

58
00:04:09.310 --> 00:04:20.970
Uncles and cousins and all had animals, and whoever's cow or calf was on the brand, they would brand them with that.

59
00:04:20.970 --> 00:04:25.740
Then with the brand that the mama cow had.

60
00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:31.570
It was great to have that kind of freedom and adventure.

61
00:04:31.570 --> 00:04:38.990
You'd work and you'd complain about, I had an uncle that ran a farm,

62
00:04:38.990 --> 00:04:49.060
and we would work and my brother, my uncles, and all, all the boys would work on the farm.

63
00:04:49.060 --> 00:04:54.890
He said, they told us they was educating us about ignorance.

64
00:04:54.890 --> 00:05:05.490
At the time, six, seven years old, you're doing things and really not grasping what they're trying to tell you.

65
00:05:05.490 --> 00:05:14.500
Then later on in life you catch on to what they're saying about educating you about ignorance.

66
00:05:14.500 --> 00:05:27.750
If you don't get a good education, that you going to have to work hard all your life and you don't want to do that.

67
00:05:27.750 --> 00:05:39.940
Once you realize what they was trying to tell you, you kind of went back to school and stayed on the honor roll.

68
00:05:39.940 --> 00:05:41.000
<v MUNGIN>[laughs]

69
00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:43.540
<v LEE>I finished like at Mossville.

70
00:05:43.540 --> 00:05:52.683
I was president of my senior class and vice-president of the student council, captain of the basketball team.

71
00:05:52.683 --> 00:05:59.794
It opened my eyes at an early age that you needed a good education.

72
00:05:59.794 --> 00:06:04.873
I pushed my children, all of them went to college.

73
00:06:04.873 --> 00:06:14.524
One of them, my oldest daughter has a doctor's in education, and she's presently superintendent of education in Smithsville,

74
00:06:14.524 --> 00:06:22.651
Virginia. [Lee clarifies that she was superintendent of Isle of Wright County Schools in Smithfield, Virginia].

75
00:06:22.651 --> 00:06:28.238
My baby girl is a RN, and she has two children.

76
00:06:28.238 --> 00:06:32.810
She's back in school to be a nurse practitioner.

77
00:06:32.810 --> 00:06:42.460
My son, he went to school, to college, and he works for the tax office here in Calcasieu Parish.

78
00:06:42.460 --> 00:06:43.476
Tax assessor.

79
00:06:43.476 --> 00:06:45.000
<v MUNGIN>Wow, congratulations.

80
00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:52.382
<v LEE>Yeah, and I tried the same fear that my uncle and them did with us.

81
00:06:52.382 --> 00:06:53.858
Educating about ignorance.

82
00:06:53.858 --> 00:06:55.827
It seemed to work.

83
00:06:55.827 --> 00:06:57.795
My brother finished college.

84
00:06:57.795 --> 00:07:00.748
Education is the key to success.

85
00:07:00.748 --> 00:07:05.669
<v MUNGIN>And was education. . . So your uncles told you about education.

86
00:07:05.669 --> 00:07:07.638
Did your parents also. . .

87
00:07:07.638 --> 00:07:08.622
<v LEE>Yeah, yeah!

88
00:07:08.622 --> 00:07:17.972
Well, my dad, man, if you didn't make the honor roll, you had to meet the strap, you understand?

89
00:07:17.972 --> 00:07:19.449
He stressed it.

90
00:07:19.449 --> 00:07:20.433
Stressed education.

91
00:07:20.433 --> 00:07:25.354
And athletics and education would get you out of here.

92
00:07:25.354 --> 00:07:28.799
Athletics would help you get a scholarship.

93
00:07:28.799 --> 00:07:31.752
It was one of the things

94
00:07:31.752 --> 00:07:33.720
<c.p>that we strived for.

95
00:07:33.720 --> 00:07:37.165
And, we seem to have accomplished it.

96
00:07:37.165 --> 00:07:46.024
Now, look back on it, you didn’t want to meet Richard Lee’s straps, so you got those books.

97
00:07:46.024 --> 00:07:56.850
My uncle, Shelton Jordan, was one of the first ones started with that education about ignorance, and it seemed to caught on.

98
00:07:56.850 --> 00:08:04.232
My dad and them would say the same thing, my grandfather and all of them.

99
00:08:04.232 --> 00:08:06.693
They just played a bass,

100
00:08:06.693 --> 00:08:17.520
it was something along the beat of a bass drum in the back of your head when you were thinking about slacking.

101
00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:24.409
I'm going to hang out with the boys and cut up and all that,

102
00:08:24.409 --> 00:08:32.283
and you out there and you think about what they told you, and you’d look around.

103
00:08:32.283 --> 00:08:33.268
Look around.

104
00:08:33.268 --> 00:08:43.110
It don't take a rocket science to see that you need that education, and, it seemed to work for us.

105
00:08:43.110 --> 00:08:47.047
<v MUNGIN>So where did you go to school again?

106
00:08:47.047 --> 00:08:49.016
<v LEE>I went to Mossville.

107
00:08:49.016 --> 00:08:50.000
<v MUNGIN>Elementary?

108
00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:51.908
<v LEE>And all through school.

109
00:08:51.908 --> 00:08:56.678
I went from first grade to twelfth grade, to Mossville.

110
00:08:56.678 --> 00:09:00.017
I came, I graduated in '66 [1966].

111
00:09:00.017 --> 00:09:06.695
And I went to Grambling, and in '68 I got drafted in the military.

112
00:09:06.695 --> 00:09:09.557
I went to the military and . . .

113
00:09:09.557 --> 00:09:10.511
<v MUNGIN>Army, navy?

114
00:09:10.511 --> 00:09:10.989
<v LEE>Army.

115
00:09:10.989 --> 00:09:16.236
Combat engineer in Vietnam, 1969 to 1970, I was in Vietnam.

116
00:09:16.236 --> 00:09:20.052
And I was injured, and I came home.

117
00:09:20.052 --> 00:09:26.730
While I was in the hospital, laying up there, I thought about what my

118
00:09:26.730 --> 00:09:28.161
<c.p>. . . education about ignorance.

119
00:09:28.161 --> 00:09:38.655
There were classes that were offered to the injured and the disabled veterans while you rehabilitate, and I took advantage of it.

120
00:09:38.655 --> 00:09:45.810
I went to the postal school, and I also went to the federal investigator school.

121
00:09:45.810 --> 00:09:56.782
I came out, after I came out the service, I worked for the postal office, for the federal government at the post office.

122
00:09:56.782 --> 00:10:09.184
Then I went to work at the sheriff's office, and that’s it. [Lee worked for the patrol department and eventually became the resident deputy of Mossville]

123
00:10:09.184 --> 00:10:13.000
<v MUNGIN>So you worked at the sheriff's office?

124
00:10:13.000 --> 00:10:19.244
<v LEE>I worked twenty-one years for Calcasieu Parish Sheriff's Office, and I worked twenty-one years at Citgo.

125
00:10:19.244 --> 00:10:21.195
I came back from Vietnam.

126
00:10:21.195 --> 00:10:25.488
I went to school, and was working at the post office.

127
00:10:25.488 --> 00:10:30.561
I couldn't sleep, so I had . . . They had union trouble out at Citgo,

128
00:10:30.561 --> 00:10:35.244
so the sheriff sent some of us out there to work and [?].

129
00:10:35.244 --> 00:10:43.049
And to try to keep down the trouble, and there, I was out there and Citgo offered me a job.

130
00:10:43.049 --> 00:10:48.122
So I took the job and I went to talk to the sheriff.

131
00:10:48.122 --> 00:10:51.634
He said, "Well, I'm going to make an exception.

132
00:10:51.634 --> 00:10:55.146
You're a good deputy, and I need good deputies.”

133
00:10:55.146 --> 00:10:59.049
And he said, “You work out a schedule with Citgo,

134
00:10:59.049 --> 00:11:06.073
and you work out a schedule with me and turn it in, and we'll see how it works.

135
00:11:06.073 --> 00:11:11.927
And we might, we want to keep you on, we don't want to lose you."

136
00:11:11.927 --> 00:11:15.049
I worked both of those jobs, twenty-one years.

137
00:11:15.049 --> 00:11:15.439
<v MUNGIN>Wow.

138
00:11:15.439 --> 00:11:17.000
<v LEE>Sixteen hours a day.

139
00:11:17.000 --> 00:11:18.561
Because I couldn't sleep.

140
00:11:18.561 --> 00:11:21.293
I had PTSD [Post Traumatic Stress Disorder].

141
00:11:21.293 --> 00:11:24.415
Back then, they didn’t know what that was.

142
00:11:24.415 --> 00:11:24.805
<v MUNGIN>Yeah.

143
00:11:24.805 --> 00:11:30.659
<v LEE>Because I was injured that night in Vietnam, and then several other people got hurt.

144
00:11:30.659 --> 00:11:33.000
<v MUNGIN>What happened, may I ask?

145
00:11:33.000 --> 00:11:38.516
<v LEE>I really wouldn't like to go into it too much.

146
00:11:38.516 --> 00:11:42.929
I also got injured, and it disabled me.

147
00:11:42.929 --> 00:11:56.167
As older I got, the more problems it gave me, so I eventually became one hundred percent disabled and I had to stop working.

148
00:11:56.167 --> 00:12:04.440
I had to go back and do some more surgery on my leg and foot.

149
00:12:04.440 --> 00:12:16.024
Well, anyway, I don't like to talk about it too much. [sighs] It was just one of those things that happened.

150
00:12:16.024 --> 00:12:25.952
At the time, I regretted it, and I kind of had a chip on my shoulder about it,

151
00:12:25.952 --> 00:12:35.881
but as time went and aged and looked around at the situations, at the rest of the world.

152
00:12:35.881 --> 00:12:43.052
I got a chance to visit, to go back to the hospital injured.

153
00:12:43.052 --> 00:12:55.187
I got a chance to see Japan, Hawaii, and stuff like that, where I was in the hospital on the way back.

154
00:12:55.187 --> 00:12:59.048
I say, "Oh, America's not that bad.

155
00:12:59.048 --> 00:13:00.151
It's great."

156
00:13:00.151 --> 00:13:04.012
I was bitter because I got drafted.

157
00:13:04.012 --> 00:13:11.183
During the sixties, there was a lot of, how would you say it?

158
00:13:11.183 --> 00:13:18.353
A lot of controversy going on about the black and white thing here,

159
00:13:18.353 --> 00:13:26.075
and you go over there and Hannah was on the radio with that music.

160
00:13:26.075 --> 00:13:31.591
And you be playing music and then they'd butt in,

161
00:13:31.591 --> 00:13:42.623
and she'd come in there telling you that they killed five at Jackson State, "What are you doing, black men?

162
00:13:42.623 --> 00:13:45.381
This is not your war."

163
00:13:45.381 --> 00:13:55.310
I mean, all that in your mind, you're going through all of that and it's a tough knot.

164
00:13:55.310 --> 00:14:09.651
Then you come back home, and you see some of that going, some of what she was saying, and it kind of spoiled in your stomach.

165
00:14:09.651 --> 00:14:14.063
But that was the sign of the times,

166
00:14:14.063 --> 00:14:17.373
<c.p>America was going through a transition.

167
00:14:17.373 --> 00:14:25.647
You know, it took a little longer in some places for it to take place.

168
00:14:25.647 --> 00:14:30.611
But, I was raised here, in this all-black community.

169
00:14:30.611 --> 00:14:38.885
We had contact with whites, and it wasn't that bad, you know what I'm saying?

170
00:14:38.885 --> 00:14:44.401
My grandfather, my dad's dad . . . when it wasn't that bad.

171
00:14:44.401 --> 00:14:51.571
Because a lot of that stuff that went on, I really didn't see.

172
00:14:51.571 --> 00:14:56.536
I seen the signs, but it didn't bother me.

173
00:14:56.536 --> 00:15:03.706
It just, it was a hell of a time to grow up in.

174
00:15:03.706 --> 00:15:08.119
And to live to see a black president.

175
00:15:08.119 --> 00:15:11.429
It can only happen in America.

176
00:15:11.429 --> 00:15:15.841
I really enjoyed my life here, in Mossville.

177
00:15:15.841 --> 00:15:24.667
Blessings, I've enjoyed my blessings that I've received throughout life, and things that I have created.

178
00:15:24.667 --> 00:15:30.183
And I got bought out, I moved up, I upgraded.

179
00:15:30.183 --> 00:15:36.250
Conoco bought me out the first time, sixteen, seventeen years ago.

180
00:15:36.250 --> 00:15:42.869
Now, I'm cashing in on my mom's property, which helps a lot.

181
00:15:42.869 --> 00:15:48.385
I upgraded my place, where I'm at now, some more.

182
00:15:48.385 --> 00:15:51.143
It's just been a blessing.

183
00:15:51.143 --> 00:15:54.452
Like I tell, it's been good.

184
00:15:54.452 --> 00:16:06.036
What's good for me might not be good for you, and what's good for you might not be good for me.

185
00:16:06.036 --> 00:16:11.000
But I really, I think it's a blessing.

186
00:16:11.000 --> 00:16:17.139
<v MUNGIN>So, to kind of backtrack a little bit, so where did you grow up?

187
00:16:17.139 --> 00:16:19.332
Where was your family's house?

188
00:16:19.332 --> 00:16:23.279
<v LEE>On Old Spanish Trail, right here on Prater [Road].

189
00:16:23.279 --> 00:16:24.156
Around Prater.

190
00:16:24.156 --> 00:16:33.365
My grandmother and them owned that whole block, like right behind, right at the corner of Prater and Old Spanish Trail.

191
00:16:33.365 --> 00:16:40.381
My grandmother's house was just west of there, and my uncle's house was south of there.

192
00:16:40.381 --> 00:16:47.398
I mean, it was my mom's house because my uncle didn't have a house back then.

193
00:16:47.398 --> 00:16:51.344
Because he was a couple years older than me.

194
00:16:51.344 --> 00:16:55.730
My mom's house was in the back here, and my

195
00:16:55.730 --> 00:16:57.045
<c.p>other auntie here.

196
00:16:57.045 --> 00:17:00.992
It was just a little family block, you know?

197
00:17:00.992 --> 00:17:06.254
Everybody was your uncle, auntie, your cousin, and I grew up here.

198
00:17:06.254 --> 00:17:06.693
And . . .

199
00:17:06.693 --> 00:17:13.270
<v MUNGIN>So your property, it was a pretty big property that you guys grew up on.

200
00:17:13.270 --> 00:17:17.656
<v LEE>Yeah, well, yeah my grandfather had a pretty good piece.

201
00:17:17.656 --> 00:17:23.795
My grandfather on my dad's side had two hundred acres down there by PPG.

202
00:17:23.795 --> 00:17:33.004
The old Jim Lee subdivision that came all the way up from like, from the [Calcasieu] River, to the Highway 90.

203
00:17:33.004 --> 00:17:42.213
But my grandfather and them here, they must have going about two or three, about three or four acres of property.

204
00:17:42.213 --> 00:17:44.844
Because, that's several houses on it.

205
00:17:44.844 --> 00:17:53.176
Yeah, I guess, it seems like more than that when you're growing corn and working on it, you know?

206
00:17:53.176 --> 00:17:58.000
<v MUNGIN>Yeah, so you guys had like, vegetables and a garden?

207
00:17:58.000 --> 00:17:59.669
<v LEE>Oh yes, oh yes.

208
00:17:59.669 --> 00:18:03.007
You had a vegetable garden, you had animals.

209
00:18:03.007 --> 00:18:05.093
<v MUNGIN>So what did you have?

210
00:18:05.093 --> 00:18:10.934
<v LEE>My grandfather grew corn, purple peas, and all sorts, tomatoes, all kinds of vegetables.

211
00:18:10.934 --> 00:18:15.940
We also had chickens, had several hogs, and a cow or two.

212
00:18:15.940 --> 00:18:20.530
A little farm, I guess you could call it a farm,

213
00:18:20.530 --> 00:18:28.457
but it was a little truck patch where you could get vegetables and pear trees and stuff like that.

214
00:18:28.457 --> 00:18:30.543
You could preserve, pickle them.

215
00:18:30.543 --> 00:18:33.881
My grandmother would put them up for preserves.

216
00:18:33.881 --> 00:18:40.139
<v MUNGIN>Did most of the other folks in this community have like, livestock and gardens too?

217
00:18:40.139 --> 00:18:50.987
<v LEE>Yeah, basically, I'd say seventy-five percent of them had some kind of little small garden or grew vegetables in the area. [portion restricted per Lee’s request]

218
00:18:50.987 --> 00:18:53.073
<v MUNGIN>Just a couple more questions.

219
00:18:53.073 --> 00:18:57.245
So who were some of the community leaders of Mossville?

220
00:18:57.245 --> 00:19:01.000
So you've been here most of your life . . .

221
00:19:01.000 --> 00:19:02.690
<v LEE>Oh, oh yeah.

222
00:19:02.690 --> 00:19:04.943
Mr. Rigmaiden, Josh Rigmaiden.

223
00:19:04.943 --> 00:19:06.070
James Rigmaiden.

224
00:19:06.070 --> 00:19:06.633
McKeever.

225
00:19:06.633 --> 00:19:08.323
You know, Edwards.

226
00:19:08.323 --> 00:19:09.449
Coach Williams.

227
00:19:09.449 --> 00:19:19.589
During my time, it was mostly like Coach Williams and James Rigmaiden were two of the most looked-up-to.

228
00:19:19.589 --> 00:19:26.911
Because Coach was our coach, and he gave us a lot of guidance.

229
00:19:26.911 --> 00:19:35.361
Family guidance, like in the, James was also a coach and a teacher at Mossville.

230
00:19:35.361 --> 00:19:40.994
You kind of already respected and looked up to them.

231
00:19:40.994 --> 00:19:48.316
And a couple of the teachers that were, like Mrs. Rogers and all.

232
00:19:48.316 --> 00:20:02.399
And there were quotes, like she would say like, "It behooves you to pay attention and understand what I'm trying to tell you, you know?

233
00:20:02.399 --> 00:20:04.652
But you not listening."

234
00:20:04.652 --> 00:20:08.032
Things like that stayed with you.

235
00:20:08.032 --> 00:20:20.424
You'd be in school, you'd be drifting off, and it would come in your head, "It would behoove you to pay attention.”

236
00:20:20.424 --> 00:20:22.114
Things like that.

237
00:20:22.114 --> 00:20:32.253
It's, man, just you could go on and on with people that touch your life in different ways.

238
00:20:32.253 --> 00:20:45.772
No one really done anything that I could remember that, at the time, you might go oh man she's mean or this, she's that.

239
00:20:45.772 --> 00:20:54.785
As you got older, you realized they was only interested in your best interest, you know?

240
00:20:54.785 --> 00:20:57.601
<c.p>We had a couple teachers.

241
00:20:57.601 --> 00:21:03.797
We used to call them "The Warden" and all of that.

242
00:21:03.797 --> 00:21:16.753
After you got to college and everything, a bunch of friends you knew from the other schools would have to take remedial subjects.

243
00:21:16.753 --> 00:21:29.146
Most of the kids from Mossville, we went and we done all the regular classes instead of having to take remedial subjects.

244
00:21:29.146 --> 00:21:31.962
You'd say, "Mossville was working."

245
00:21:31.962 --> 00:21:34.215
Coach, if you didn't,

246
00:21:34.215 --> 00:21:51.114
you had to make at least a C or B before they started all this with the grades and stuff in school about not being able to participate in sports.

247
00:21:51.114 --> 00:21:56.184
Coach Williams had already enforced these rules and regulations.

248
00:21:56.184 --> 00:21:59.000
<v MUNGIN>Before they were mandatory.

249
00:21:59.000 --> 00:21:59.461
<v LEE>Yeah.

250
00:21:59.461 --> 00:22:02.226
That was some of the things.

251
00:22:02.226 --> 00:22:08.679
We go somewhere and the bus break down, somebody ask you, "Where you at?"

252
00:22:08.679 --> 00:22:12.367
What you going to do, scratch your head?

253
00:22:12.367 --> 00:22:17.437
Pay attention to the road signs and know where you at.

254
00:22:17.437 --> 00:22:21.124
You never know when you have an emergency.

255
00:22:21.124 --> 00:22:23.429
Little simple things like that.

256
00:22:23.429 --> 00:22:28.499
In junior high, we were just cutting up on the bus.

257
00:22:28.499 --> 00:22:33.569
Every now and then, he would ask somebody where we was.

258
00:22:33.569 --> 00:22:35.873
He’d say, "Where we at?"

259
00:22:35.873 --> 00:22:41.865
And you better be able to give them an answer where you at.

260
00:22:41.865 --> 00:22:46.474
Them signs on the road is there for a purpose.

261
00:22:46.474 --> 00:22:46.935
<v MUNGIN>Yeah.

262
00:22:46.935 --> 00:22:51.084
<v LEE>It opened your eyes to little things like that.

263
00:22:51.084 --> 00:23:02.146
They’d say oh, Coach, crazy man, he wanted you to read the signs, going down the road, all that mile-marker this and all that.

264
00:23:02.146 --> 00:23:09.981
But he was all the time, he was teaching you that, putting some direction in your life.

265
00:23:09.981 --> 00:23:16.434
<v MUNGIN>So, a couple more questions and then we’re going to call it a day.

266
00:23:16.434 --> 00:23:21.504
What places were some of the more important places in Mossville?

267
00:23:21.504 --> 00:23:22.887
Like houses, buildings--

268
00:23:22.887 --> 00:23:27.496
<v LEE>Big Mama's, on Sundays or any day, was the spot.

269
00:23:27.496 --> 00:23:28.418
Big Mama.

270
00:23:28.418 --> 00:23:34.871
Sunday, you went to church, you walked to Mt. Zion and to Sunday school.

271
00:23:34.871 --> 00:23:41.784
We went to church and about every Sunday, we'd go back to Big Mama's house.

272
00:23:41.784 --> 00:23:44.089
It was a family dinner.

273
00:23:44.089 --> 00:23:51.003
The other places, this is like later on, they had the rec, the swimming pool.

274
00:23:51.003 --> 00:23:58.377
Being free to ride horses in the woods and deal with Mother Nature and camp out.

275
00:23:58.377 --> 00:24:03.908
We would load up on the Fourth of July, my great-grandfather Towner,

276
00:24:03.908 --> 00:24:11.744
we would go up on the river and camp out for the Fourth of July every year.

277
00:24:11.744 --> 00:24:23.267
That was my favorite spots, and my grandfather, my daddy's dad, back in the day when they had the black side and the white side,

278
00:24:23.267 --> 00:24:35.251
he ran the black side of the Midway [drive-in movie theater] because he sold the property for the old drive-in theater out there on Highway 90.

279
00:24:35.251 --> 00:24:41.243
One of the stipulations that they would have an area for the blacks.

280
00:24:41.243 --> 00:24:45.852
That was another place where we’d go watch the movies.

281
00:24:45.852 --> 00:24:48.156
Basically, Big Mama's House was--

282
00:24:48.156 --> 00:24:50.000
<v MUNGIN>The go-to spot.

283
00:24:50.000 --> 00:24:52.604
<v LEE>That was the spot, man.

284
00:24:52.604 --> 00:25:01.457
You go to Big Mama's house, and she's going to feed everybody, I don't care who that.

285
00:25:01.457 --> 00:25:04.061
"Where you want to go?”

286
00:25:04.061 --> 00:25:08.227
“Hey man, let's go to your grandma's house!"

287
00:25:08.227 --> 00:25:12.914
You know, Big Mama had some snacks for you.

288
00:25:12.914 --> 00:25:18.642
But you going to do the right thing over there, though.

289
00:25:18.642 --> 00:25:20.204
She was great.

290
00:25:20.204 --> 00:25:22.808
She made a big imprint.

291
00:25:22.808 --> 00:25:27.495
She was a big boot mark in my life.

292
00:25:27.495 --> 00:25:31.141
Because, parents worked, both of them worked.

293
00:25:31.141 --> 00:25:35.827
I spent a lot of time with my grandmother

294
00:25:35.827 --> 00:25:36.869
<c.p>and grandfather.

295
00:25:36.869 --> 00:25:48.847
They were very loving and understanding, and what they had rules that you had to go by, and you had to do it.

296
00:25:48.847 --> 00:25:51.971
Nobody could whup like Big Mama.

297
00:25:51.971 --> 00:25:53.013
You know--

298
00:25:53.013 --> 00:25:53.534
<v MUNGIN>Yeah.

299
00:25:53.534 --> 00:25:57.700
<v LEE>But nobody could be no kinder than her.

300
00:25:57.700 --> 00:25:58.741
You know?

301
00:25:58.741 --> 00:26:02.907
You just did what you needed to do.

302
00:26:02.907 --> 00:26:06.553
<v MUNGIN>So, last three questions then we're done.

303
00:26:06.553 --> 00:26:09.677
What does home mean to you?

304
00:26:09.677 --> 00:26:12.281
<v LEE>Man, that's a hard one.

305
00:26:12.281 --> 00:26:15.406
Because, I really never left Mossville.

306
00:26:15.406 --> 00:26:25.821
Even though I moved up to Gillis, Moss Bluff, every day just about I'd come see my mother, my grandmother.

307
00:26:25.821 --> 00:26:27.383
Then they passed.

308
00:26:27.383 --> 00:26:37.278
I still come through here and ride, because I made my living here and I'd be looking for changes.

309
00:26:37.278 --> 00:26:43.527
Seeing it disappear and what it's, I think it's a good thing.

310
00:26:43.527 --> 00:26:50.297
But, home to me is, man, I can't really put it in words,

311
00:26:50.297 --> 00:27:00.712
I couldn't give it-- There's not a word in the dictionary that, to me, that would really describe, you know?

312
00:27:00.712 --> 00:27:02.796
And, I love Mossville.

313
00:27:02.796 --> 00:27:05.399
The area and the people.

314
00:27:05.399 --> 00:27:08.003
I made my living here.

315
00:27:08.003 --> 00:27:13.211
I wouldn't want to try to put it to words.

316
00:27:13.211 --> 00:27:24.668
No more than what I said that because I couldn't give it the justice, with one word, what home means to me.

317
00:27:24.668 --> 00:27:33.000
<v MUNGIN>This one might be a little more difficult, but what does community mean to you?

318
00:27:33.000 --> 00:27:33.500
<v LEE>Family.

319
00:27:33.500 --> 00:27:37.000
Because Mossville was a community of family.

320
00:27:37.000 --> 00:27:41.000
It was somewhere, someone, they all were connected.

321
00:27:41.000 --> 00:27:45.000
You start seeing it disappear with the school

322
00:27:45.000 --> 00:27:56.000
<c.p>closing, the churches separating, and this that and the other, and the people, community, parts of being sold out and bought out.

323
00:27:56.000 --> 00:28:04.500
But, change-- Who am I to say it's wrong, and who am I to say it's right?

324
00:28:04.500 --> 00:28:16.500
Like again I say, "What's good for you might not be good for me, and what's good for me might not be. . ." Vice versa.

325
00:28:16.500 --> 00:28:23.000
<v MUNGIN>What's the most important thing you want people to remember about Mossville?

326
00:28:23.000 --> 00:28:28.900
<v LEE>[whistles] Man, I'm getting cold chills on that one.

327
00:28:28.900 --> 00:28:29.556
Man.

328
00:28:29.556 --> 00:28:32.178
That it was home.

329
00:28:32.178 --> 00:28:34.144
It was everything.

330
00:28:34.144 --> 00:28:36.111
Family and friends.

331
00:28:36.111 --> 00:28:42.011
It just, kindness and goodness in the people's heart.

332
00:28:42.011 --> 00:28:47.256
During the times that we grew up here.

333
00:28:47.256 --> 00:28:50.533
It's, that's my fond memories.

334
00:28:50.533 --> 00:28:51.844
Again that's [. . .?].

335
00:28:51.844 --> 00:28:54.467
<v MUNGIN>Yeah, I got you.

336
00:28:54.467 --> 00:29:01.022
Is there anything else you want to add in closing?

337
00:29:01.022 --> 00:29:02.989
<v LEE>Not really, man.

338
00:29:02.989 --> 00:29:09.544
You touched base about on everything I had to say.

339
00:29:09.544 --> 00:29:25.278
All I can tell you is I hope Sasol has success and I'm proud to say that I once lived there, but it hurts.

340
00:29:25.278 --> 00:29:25.933
<v MUNGIN>Yeah.

341
00:29:25.933 --> 00:29:32.489
So that's going to wrap up our time for today.

342
00:29:32.489 --> 00:29:38.389
I want to thank you so much for participating.

343
00:29:38.389 --> 00:29:46.911
I know a lot of issues kind of come up during these interviews,

344
00:29:46.911 --> 00:30:01.333
so I just really want to thank you so much for your time and for just breaking it down for us today.

345
00:30:01.333 --> 00:30:05.267
<v LEE>Yeah, it's a blessing in disguise.

346
00:30:05.267 --> 00:30:07.233
In a way.

347
00:30:07.233 --> 00:30:15.100
But, you know, you can always keep it here, in your heart.

348
00:30:15.100 --> 00:30:21.000
<v MUNGIN>Alright, so that's going to conclude our interview.

349
00:30:21.000 --> 00:30:21.429
<c.p>[End Tape 4408.

350
00:30:21.429 --> 00:30:21.857
End Session I.]
